  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1149 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThe upper-level trough responsible for active weather across west \ncentral Texas on Thursday will start to shift eastward on Friday \ninto Saturday, leaving behind shortwave ridging across Texas. A \nstrong surface cold front will also push southward through central \nTexas by Friday morning with a 1033mb high pressure center behind \nit.  The strong pressure gradient should produce strong winds behind \nthe front and wind speeds could reach 20-30 MPH on Friday.  At this \npoint, there is a low to medium (30-60%) chance for winds exceeding \n30 MPH at some point on Friday, which would meet Wind Advisory \ncriteria.  Otherwise, much cooler and more stable air will settle in \nacross the region for the weekend. High temperatures look to be in \nthe low to mid 60s for Friday and Saturday but could feel a bit \ncooler on Friday due to lingering cloud cover. Skies should clear \nout on Friday night with light winds which should allow for ideal \nradiational cooling conditions early Saturday morning as well as \nSunday morning.  As a result, low temperatures both mornings could \nbe quite cool as they look to drop to around 40. This would be the \ncoldest temperatures we have experienced since mid-April!\n\nThe strong area of high pressure will move eastward by Sunday \nafternoon and allow for southerly return flow at the surface while \nan upper-level shortwave passes to the north. High temperatures \nshould climb back into the 70s on Sunday and Monday and into the 80s \non Tuesday. The upper-level pattern could become more active for the \nmiddle part of next week as global models are in fairly good \nagreement of an upper-level low digging into southern California by \nTuesday night.  This would place much of west Texas under southwest \nflow aloft, similar to what occurred earlier this week. Differences \nin timing and moisture amounts still exist on this feature so for \nnow, low (20-30%) chances for rain are in the forecast for Wednesday.",
